To ensure the local BGP instance's interface address is propagated as the next-hop for routes advertised to the third-party AS neighbor, use the `next-hop-self` command. Configure this at the appropriate BGP group or neighbor level:

```cli
configure router bgp group <group_name>
    next-hop-self
exit
```

If applied to a specific neighbor instead of the entire group:
```cli
configure router bgp group <group_name> neighbor <neighbor_IP>
    next-hop-self
exit
```

This forces all advertised routes to use the BGP instance's local interface address (e.g., loopback) as the next-hop, overriding the default physical interface address. Existing route policies remain unaffected. 

**Note for IPv6 Scenarios**:  
If advertising IPv4 VPN or labeled routes over an IPv6 BGP session and requiring an IPv6 next-hop, additionally configure:
```cli
configure router bgp group <group_name>
    advertise-ipv6-next-hops vpn-ipv4 label-ipv4
exit
```
Replace `vpn-ipv4 label-ipv4` with the specific address families relevant to your deployment.
